<head>
  <title>Azox — The Sound of Future Web</title>
  <meta name="description" content="A web framework with fine-grained signal reactivity and no Virtual DOM. Components compile straight to DOM operations." />
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/style.css" />
  <script src="/site.js" defer></script>
</head>

<script>
  import Nav from '../components/Nav.azox';
  import Footer from '../components/Footer.azox';
  import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.azox';
  import { signal } from 'azox/reactivity';

  const count = signal(0);
</script>

<div class="shell">
  <Nav version="0.1.0" />

  <header class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <span class="eyebrow">v0.1.0 · early development</span>
      <h1>Azox<br /><span class="hero-tagline">The Sound of Future Web</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">
        A web framework built from scratch. No Virtual DOM, no diffing, no
        runtime dependencies. Every binding compiles to a signal that writes
        to exactly one DOM node.
      </p>

      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/docs">Get started</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/playground">Try the playground</a>
      </div>

      <div class="install-line">
        <span class="install-prompt">$</span>
        <span>npx azoxjs create my-app</span>
        <button class="copy-btn" data-copy-text="npx azoxjs create my-app">Copy</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2 class="section-title">See it work</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">
      The counter below is a real Azox page, compiled with the same
      compiler you install. Clicking updates one text node — nothing
      else on this page is touched.
    </p>

    <div class="demo">
      <div class="demo-stage">
        <span class="demo-readout">{count()}</span>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <button class="btn btn-primary" on:click={() => count.set(count() + 1)}>Add one</button>
          <button class="btn btn-ghost" on:click={() => count.set(0)}>Reset</button>
        </div>
        <p class="demo-note">
          No Virtual DOM was consulted. The effect bound to that number
          is the only thing that ran.
        </p>
      </div>

      <CodeBlock label="pages/index.azox"><text><script>
  import { signal } from 'azox/reactivity';

  const count = signal(0);
</script>

<span class="readout">{count()}</span>

<button on:click={() => count.set(count() + 1)}>
  Add one
</button></text></CodeBlock>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2 class="section-title">Why it is different</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">
      Most frameworks re-run your component and compare the result.
      Azox compiles the comparison away entirely.
    </p>

    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="card-icon">01</p>
        <h3>Fine-grained by default</h3>
        <p>
          Each binding becomes its own effect, holding a direct reference to
          the text node or attribute it owns. A signal change writes to that
          node and stops.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <p class="card-icon">02</p>
        <h3>Components vanish at build time</h3>
        <p>
          A component is inlined into its caller. There is no component
          instance at runtime, no lifecycle, and nothing to reconcile — the
          output matches hand-written markup.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <p class="card-icon">03</p>
        <h3>Zero dependencies</h3>
        <p>
          The CLI, the compiler and the runtime are plain JavaScript against
          the standard library. Nothing is pulled in behind your back.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <p class="card-icon">04</p>
        <h3>Its own syntax</h3>
        <p>
          <code>.azox</code> files are HTML with <code>{'{'}expr{'}'}</code>
          interpolation and <code>on:event</code> bindings. Not JSX, not a
          template DSL to memorise.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <p class="card-icon">05</p>
        <h3>Server-rendered first</h3>
        <p>
          Every page ships real HTML. The client module then wires up the
          bindings on top of it, so content is there before JavaScript runs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card">
        <p class="card-icon">06</p>
        <h3>Static output, anywhere</h3>
        <p>
          A build is plain HTML, JS and assets with clean URLs. It works on
          any static host with no rewrite rules or adapters.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2 class="section-title">What a project looks like</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">
      The file layout is the routing table, and there is no configuration
      file to learn.
    </p>

    <div class="demo">
      <CodeBlock label="project"><text>my-app/
├── pages/
│   ├── index.azox      →  /
│   ├── about.azox      →  /about
│   └── blog/
│       ├── index.azox  →  /blog
│       └── first.azox  →  /blog/first
├── components/
│   └── Card.azox
└── public/
    └── style.css       →  /style.css</text></CodeBlock>

      <CodeBlock label="components/Card.azox"><text><script>
  const { title, body } = props();
</script>

<article class="card">
  <h2>{title}</h2>
  <p>{body}</p>
</article></text></CodeBlock>
    </div>

    <p class="muted">
      Read the <a href="/docs">documentation</a> to go further, or open the
      <a href="/playground">playground</a> to compile a page in your browser.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Footer />
</div>
